Every page load creates a new `window` object. You only stub the first visit window, thus the second page visit creates a new `window` object, without your stub. See all answers to your question in https://glebbahmutov.com/blog/stub-window-open/ but in general

```js
// ✅ CORRECT SOLUTION
it('opens a new window', () => {
  // create a single stub we will use
  const stub = cy.stub().as('open')
  cy.on('window:before:load', (win) => {
    cy.stub(win, 'open').callsFake(stub)
  })

  cy.visit('/')
  // triggers the application to call window.open
  cy.click('Open new window')
  cy.get('@open').should('have.been.calledOnce')

  // cause the window to be recreated
  cy.reload()
  cy.click('Open new window')
  // all window.open calls are correctly forwarded to our stub
  cy.get('@open').should('have.been.calledTwice')
})
```

I have managed a workaround thanks to [this article](https://www.artmann.co/articles/waiting-for-network-resources-in-cypress). My code is modified for my needs but I think this function should be good enough for most use cases. It basically polls the browser's resources to see if it's still loading something or not. It checks every 2 seconds and will resolve the promise when it has seen the same thing 3 times (no new progress). You can also pass it an array of specific resources that you want to wait for and the number of each resource that you require.

```js
Cypress.Commands.add("waitForResources", function (resources = []) {
	const globalTimeout = 20000
	const resourceCheckInterval = 2000
	const idleTimesInit = 3
	let idleTimes = idleTimesInit
	let resourcesLengthPrevious
	let timeout

	return new Cypress.Promise((resolve, reject) => {
		const checkIfResourcesLoaded = () => {
			const resourcesLoaded = cy.state("window")
				.performance.getEntriesByType("resource")
				.filter(r => !["script", "xmlhttprequest"].includes(r.initiatorType))

			const allFilesFound = resources.every(
				resource => {
					const found = resourcesLoaded.filter(
						resourceLoaded => {
							return resourceLoaded.name.includes(resource.name)
						},
					)
					if (found.length === 0) {
						return false
					}
					return !resource.number || found.length >= resource.number
				},
			)

			if (allFilesFound) {
				if (resourcesLoaded.length === resourcesLengthPrevious) {
					idleTimes--
				}
				else {
					idleTimes = idleTimesInit
					resourcesLengthPrevious = resourcesLoaded.length
				}
			}
			if (!idleTimes) {
				resolve()
				return
			}

			timeout = setTimeout(checkIfResourcesLoaded, resourceCheckInterval)
		}

		checkIfResourcesLoaded()
		setTimeout(() => {
			reject()
			clearTimeout(timeout)
		}, globalTimeout)
	})
})
```

usage:
```js
cy.waitForResources() // wait for networkidle
cy.waitForResources([
	{ name: "fa-solid-900.woff2" },
	{ name: "fonts.gstatic.com/s/worksans", number: 2 }, // won't resolve until it has loaded 2 matching resources
])
```

Hi @johannescpk,

We just released Cypress 7.0, which should give you more flexibility with configuring Cypress for component testing.

7.0 comes with a completely revamped Component Testing experience. Component testing is no longer experimental (but still in alpha, with lots of updates and improvements coming in the next few weeks). Please let us know if you find any more issues.

Updated Component Testing intro: https://docs.cypress.io/guides/component-testing/introduction
Changelog: https://docs.cypress.io/guides/references/changelog
Migration guide: https://docs.cypress.io/guides/references/migration-guide#Component-Testing

The main difference is we moved from a `file:preprocessor` architecture to a dev-server architecture. Component testing is no longer experimental, and has a dedicated runner (which is build on top of an extends the existing cypress runner).

The main benefits of the improved component testing runner and architecture are:

- things are now (much) faster
- no need to launch into the electron shell. It opens straight to the browser (select your spec from the inline spec selector)

Regarding your specific request, the way we recommend most people to inject the dev-server (previously file:preprocessor) is like this:

```js
// cypress/plugins/index.js

const injectDevServer = require("@cypress/react/plugins/react-scripts")

module.exports = (on, config) => {
  injectDevServer(on, config)
  return config
}
```

If you want more flexibility (like you do), you can see how [the plugin works](https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/blob/develop/npm/react/plugins/react-scripts/index.js)

```js
const { startDevServer } = require('@cypress/webpack-dev-server')
const findReactScriptsWebpackConfig = require('./findReactScriptsWebpackConfig')

module.exports = (on, config) => {
  on('dev-server:start', async (options) => {
    return startDevServer({ 
      options, 
      webpackConfig: findReactScriptsWebpackConfig(config) 
    })
  })

  // IMPORTANT to return the config object
  // with the any changed environment variables
  return config
}
```

So what you should be able to do is:

1. find your react-scripts webpack config (using `find-webpack` or [this function](https://github.com/cypress-io/cypress/blob/develop/npm/react/plugins/react-scripts/findReactScriptsWebpackConfig.js))
2. in `cypress/plugins/index.js`, when you do the `dev-server:start` event and pass in your `webpackConfig`, modify the webpack config there
3. This should allow you to do whatever modifications you see fit.

You will need Cypess 7.x and the new dev-server architecture and dedicated component testing runner (which you open with `yarn cypress open-ct`, [see the migration docs for more information](https://docs.cypress.io/guides/references/migration-guide#Component-Testing).

Lot of information, let me know if you need help with updating, or if you run into any problems.

