@ScottAgirs `cypress run` is a very basic way of running tests. As your project grows bigger you will need a lot of different custom configuration to execute your tests with. 

Its better to use [Cypress Module API](https://docs.cypress.io/guides/guides/module-api) to programatically run your tests using node process. 

create a `cypress.run.js` file in your project and an npm script in your `package.json` like so: 
`{ "cy:run": "node cypress.run.js"}` and call it using npm or yarn : `npm run cy:run`

Pro-Tip: You can have multiple cypress.run.js files for different environments or setups with different configuration and have a separate script for each in your package.json
``` json
{
  "cy:run:smoke:tests": "node cypress.smoke.run.js",
  "cy:run:staging": "node cypress.staging.js",
  "cy:run:e2e": "node cypress.e2e.js",
  "cy:run:production": "node cypress.production.js"
}
```

The magic happens in your `cypress.run.js` file where you can pass all sorts of custom config and arguments(refer module api docs for more info)

In your case, if you want to run a specific set of tests, you can pass an array of all the files under the `config` object of the `options` that is needed by `cypress.run(options)` or can directly pass it to the `spec` property of the `options`. See example below.

see documentation for `config` object: https://docs.cypress.io/guides/references/configuration#Folders-Files

```javascript
//cypress.run.js

// you can either use the `spec` property or `config.testFiles` property in runOptions. Your choice...

; (async () => {
  const runOptions = {
    headless: true,
    parallel: true,
    record: true,
    browser: 'chrome',
    spec: './cypress/integration/examples/actions.spec.js' // refer module api docs
    config: {
      testFiles: '**/*.spec.js' // refer configuration docs
    }
  }
  await cypress
    .run(runOptions)
    .then((results) => {
      console.log(results)
    })
    .catch((err) => {
      console.error(err)
    })
})()
```
