Happy to be corrected by an actual nextjs developer, but my understanding from peeking around in the source code is that both webpack4 and webpack5 are vendored into the next source and it selects the one required at runtime. It then hooks into the require mechanism so `requre('webpack')` points to the bundled nextjs version. If you add webpack as your own dependency, then you seem to end up with two instances of webpack in play at the same time which causes some unpredictable behaviour.
The special sauce is [here](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/tree/canary/packages/next/build/webpack)
As far as I can tell the two options are
1 - start with a base webpack config, modify it if necessary using the webpack() function from the users `next.config.js` file and start the webpack devserver from that:

    const baseConfig=require('./baseWebPackConfig')
    const webpack=require('webpack')
    const nextConfig=require(path.join(projectRoot,'next.config'))

    const nextWebPackConfig=nextConfig.webpack(baseConfig,{webpack})
    const webpackCompiler=webpack(nextWebPackConfig)
    if (nextConfig.future?.webpack5 && webpack.version().startsWith('4.')) throw 'you need to use the same version of webpack as specified in your next.config.js file'
    ...

2 - use the actual next devserver and run the cypress component testing framework as a next app. This requires a bit of hackery as you need to bypass any __app component that the next app might have but is doable. I see there is a PR which should fix this issue already but will submit my server-as-a-next-app version as a PR anyway (because I might as well)

