This is because the `binary` encoding... is not actually binary. It is an alias for `latin1', [same as in node](https://nodejs.org/api/buffer.html#buffer_buffers_and_character_encodings).

For working with binary data in cypress, we instead suggest using the `null` encoding, which will probably behave more like you expect:

```
describe('Various tests', () => {
	it('Downloads file', () => {
		cy.request({
			url: 'http://localhost/tilde.txt',
			encoding: null
		}).then(response => {
      console.log(Cypress.Buffer.from(response.body).toString())
			cy.writeFile('cypress/documents/tilde-request.txt', Cypress.Buffer.from(response.body), null);
			cy.wrap(Cypress.Buffer.from(response.body).toString()).should('eq', 'This character: "\\~" is called a tilde.');
		})
	})
})
```

Note how it now has "\~" in the string - it's dealing with the data straight off the disk, without interpreting "\~" as an escaped tilde.

With that said, it would be good for consistency's sake if `cy.request` returned a Cypress.Buffer for `null` encoding, same as `cy.readFile()` and `cy.fixture()` - but at the moment it returns an ArrayBuffer, which you can either work with directly or turn into a Cypress.Buffer as is convenient.

